Dear S-users,

a little while ago I got a function from Bill Dunlap to put on any axis all the
labels I wanted.  The function works fine excepted for a little thing:  most of
the time all the labels are positioned correctly on the axis, I often passed the
adj parameter to have the labels justified as I want, BUT once in while for
unknown reasons out of ten or more plots coming out of a function where I call
Bill Dunlaps function one or two plot will have one or two axis labels
completely off, when I say off I mean off, i.e. by one or two inches?  Any
clues?  "my.axis" if called from other functions, is there anything I should do
to insure that all the par's setting remain the same between calls?

Thanks for your help,

"my.axis" <- function(side, at, labels, ...)
{
# Author  : Bill Dunlap from Mathsoft
# Date    : October 1999
# Purpose : To put labels at desired "tick marks" on the desired axis.
# Parameters :
#  side   : the desired side for the axis.
#  at     : the desired positions for the "tick marks" and the labels.
#  labels : the desired labels for the "tick marks".
#  ...    : other parameters that could be passed to axis.

  for(i in seq(along=at))
    axis(side=side, at=at[i], labels=labels[i], ...)
}

Solution:
=======

 After a bit, too much, of fooling around I found the culpright.  The x-axis
labels were factor levels and I noticed that for all the off position labels the
character "|" was part of the label generated from the factor level.  I was
using the "|" in some of the factor levels for a short of "or" (for exemple:
"this or that" was coded as "this | that").  I suppose that the "|" is used as
some carriage control character or tab or something?  As soon as I changed the
factor levels where the "|" was appearing all was fine.

